Focusing on carburetor and the combustion chamber:

1. The narrowing, nozzle-like geometry of venturi accelerates intake fluid and decreases its pressure

2. Fuel is drawn into the carburetor at this low pressure region

3. Piston strokes draw fluid into the combustion chamber and compress it

4. Spark plug ignites the fluid and the explosion restarts the process


The fluid ends up doing some interesting things:

- Before the throat → High P, Accelerating Fluid

- At throat → Low P, Highest Velocity

- After throat → New Density, Velocity Decrease 



<h2>Model</h2>
The main models we use for our analysis are incompressible, isentropic and steady flow. Incompressible flow assumes that as pressure and velocity change, density does not and the velocities throughout are below Mach .3, so this is a valid assumption. The isentropic model states that there is no major heat or viscous losses which also implies inviscid flow; we will use this model only for air. Steady flow assumes that flow is time-invariant yielding mass conservation. 

